hello and welcome to this presentation  on the journey of a sample through an  automated lcms  ms workflow my name is robert wardle  and i'm a senior scientist at waters  corporation  where my role involves the development  and support of applications  on our lcms ms systems and i also  focus on automating these workflows  so today i will go through why you would  want to focus on  automation for lcm sms workflows  we will take a look at the waters  automation portfolio  and then it will go on a journey of the  sample  through this workflow before finally  looking at an example  for a panel of steroid hormones which is  an  application on progestogens and  androgens  once a sample is received into a  laboratory for lcm sms analysis  the first thing we will need to do is  perform some form of sample extraction  and the reason for this is that we need  to remove matrix interferences  this helps to increase signal to noise  by reducing chromatographic background  noise detected  in the mass spectrometer the result of  this  is an increase in analytical sensitivity  and provides  better more consistent quantification  sample extraction also reduces  variability associated  with matrix effects more specifically  iron suppression and enhancement effects  this provides more consistent data and  increased robustness of the lcms system  through improved column lifetime and  therefore reducing  costs as fewer columns will be required  replacement  it also reduces downtime associated with  cleaning of the system  and its components as the sample being  introduced to the system is much cleaner  there is also the benefit of  concentrating the analytes of interest  particularly those which are present at  low levels that can be difficult to  quantify  of course sample extraction can be time  consuming and laborious  and i know i have certainly had some  long days in the lab where it felt like  my hand has fallen off like armies  it's also prone to human error  especially when  there are more complex extraction  procedures required  and transcription errors are also  possible such as when manually typing  results into a limbs  this is why waters has focused on  automation  and looking at ways to improve the  workflow from sample receipt  through to reporting a results back into  a limbs  to minimize these problems  the evolution of the water's lcms  workflows started in the 2000s  where at this time and earlier sample  extraction was performed manually  generally with small batches of samples  analytical systems were hplc with tandem  mass spectrometers  such as the waters alliance and quattro  micro  and informatics was only really used to  control these systems  there was no sample tracking performed  with no limbs connectivity  or automation if we then went forward to  the 2010s  sample extraction was mostly still  performed manually  but was moving to larger batches and  having some liquid handling robot  functionality  analytical systems had moved on to uplc  with more sensitive tandem mass  spectrometers  allowing for faster run times and the  analysis of more complex analytes  also partial sample tracking was  possible with the introduction of the  tcam file converter software  but there was still no lims connectivity  so now let's let's focus on our current  solutions  and then take a more in-depth look at  how each of these  can be applied to the lcms workflow  we have split products up into three  categories based on sample throughput  volume  the labs running a lower number of tests  products such as the  andrew alliance pipet plus which are  electronic handheld pipettes  can provide a simple to use smart  pipette system  to help improve prepare accuracy and  minimize human error  if solid phase extraction is performed  then the auto specialist can improve  flow control  when drawing samples through and  diluting from the spe plate  with automated pressure gradients  the next step up into medium volume  workflows looks at the andrew alliance  andrew plus  liquid handling robot which automates  parts or all of the sample extraction  process  allowing for the analyst to perform  other tasks whilst samples are being  extracted  and then we come on to the higher volume  workflows with larger liquid handling  robots such as the tcam  freedom evo series or hamilton star  products  which give the highest level of  automation to the lcm sms workflows  and have the largest amount of  flexibility  another very important part of the lcms  automation workflow  is sample tracking which helps to  minimize transcription errors  and this is where the masslinx lims  interface and file converter tools come  in  and these allow for connectivity between  the limbs  mass links which is the waters lcm sms  system  software and in some cases the liquid  handling robot software  and the role of these products is to  transfer information  such as sample id well position and  results  so now i am going to take you through  our sample journey  before we look at each of the products  discussed and how they fit into this  process  the journey begins with the request for  the analysis of a sample  multiple samples are typically batched  together for the same analysis prior to  their extraction and quantification  in the case of the pipet plus no limbs  connectivity is possible currently  and therefore once the lab is ready to  perform the test  the work list with all samples to be  tested is printed  and the analyst will retrieve these  samples and perform the sample  extraction  the analysts will then place the  extracted samples onto the lcm sms  system for analysis  a massive example list can then be  created by using the masslink slim's  interface software  which i'll discuss in more detail later  and with this the sample id from the  lims  is imported and the lcm sms method  parameters from a method specific  template are set  the analyst must then specify the well  position or vial position of the samples  as with pipette plus sample extraction  is still performed manually  and once this is done the sample run is  submitted  and lcm sms quantification is performed  the final piece of the journey is the  same for all lcms workflows  once the data has been acquired the  batch is processed in target links  access software by the analyst  they can check that all acceptance  criteria has been met  such as the calibration line correlation  coefficient  and qc sample bias being within the  allowable limits  target links also has features to assist  the analyst in this process  where data is flagged if it is outside  of the preset tolerance limit  the data is then reviewed and approved  before being sent back to the lims  using the masslinx lims interface  so how does pipet plus assist in this  process  and what is different when compared to a  simple electronic pipette  well they are browser controlled  electronic pipettes  meaning that the extraction method is  programmed into the one lab software  the analyst will then execute the method  and the software will guide them step by  step  through the extraction furthermore  the volume of the pipette is set by the  software automatically  and with all and with the simple press  of a button the pipette will aspirate  the reagent  and then another press to dispense into  the required container  which again is nicely displayed in the  software  and you can just about see in the image  shown  of course as a result of this you will  get improved lab productivity and  reproducibility  results  if we compare this to the android plus  for a medium volume workflow  the two are very similar however with  andrew plus  the sample extraction is performed on a  liquid handling robot  as opposed to being done manually which  should help to improve  day-to-day precision of results reduce  human error  and allow for the analyst to perform  other tasks while sample extraction is  taking place  again the journey begins with a request  for the analysis of a sample  with samples being batched together the  analyst then retrieves the samples  and either adds them to the deck of the  andrew plus  or transfers them into a 96 well plate  for sample extraction  along with all reagents and consumables  required  as guided by the onelab software  the extraction run will be executed and  once completed  the analyst places the extracted samples  onto the lcm sms system for analysis  the remaining part of the workflow is  the same as we described the pipette  lost  with a mass linked sample list being  created using mass  the mass link's limbs interface software  and samples quantified  data is processed reviewed and approved  and sent back to the limbs again using  the masterlink's limbs interface tool  if we take a look at andrew plus in more  detail  like pipet plus it is controlled by the  onelab software  providing an easy to use visual  interface to assist with the setup to  run  the extraction method and allows full  traceability of protocols  andrew plus has the ability to perform a  range of extraction techniques  such as protein precipitation solid  phase extraction  and liquid liquid extractions with its  small footprint allowing it to be placed  in a fume hood  the analysts will load the samples which  could either be  in sample tubes or transferred into a 96  well plate  add the reagents and consumables and  then execute the protocol  in the one lab software and then whilst  the sample extraction run is taking  place  the analyst time is obviously freed up  allowing them to perform other tasks  and our final sample journey to discuss  is for higher volume workplace  where a tea can freedom evo or hamilton  spa  or starlet platforms are used  on the t on the teak and liquid handling  robots  a batch of samples is loaded onto the  liquid handling robot  from a printed work list reagents and  consumables are loaded  and checked using laboratory sops  and the script is set to run  on the hamilton star or starlet systems  there is the option to compare samples  requested from the limbs against those  loaded onto the hamilton system  and to do this the masslinx limbs  interface will create a file  listing the samples for the analysis  required  the analysts will load these samples and  the sample tube barcodes are scanned  and compared to those listed in this  worklist file  any differences between the two will  then be flagged to the analyst before  the extraction room begins  the advantage of this is that it ensures  the correct samples are extracted and  analyzed for that particular analysis  on both the tcan and hamilton systems  sample tube barcodes are scanned  and tracked through the extraction  process and so the final location of all  samples are known  once the run is complete a file is  generated  with this information present  in the case of the t-can a file  converter is used to allow the sample id  and weld position to be combined with  the templates  containing the lcm sms method  information  to create the sample list in math links  however for the hamilton all information  can be generated using  hamilton's venus software and so the  file can be generated directly with no  need for a file converter  once the sample list has been generated  the analyst transfers the plate  containing the extracted samples into  the lcms system  and as with other workflows samples are  analyzed  data processed and reported back to the  limbs  so the hamilton star and ticonfrida  t kent freedom evo systems all have the  ability to track  samples through the extraction process  from the sample tube barcode  scanned by the robot as you would  expect many different types of sample  extraction methods are possible  depending on the deck layout selected  but the options available on each of  these sim  systems is almost endless and i'm sure  you could probably make some very  precise cocktails as well if you wanted  these systems also have the ability to  perform liquid level detection  which ensures that there is a sufficient  volume of sample  and reagents to perform the method with  the volume  set in the software today  these liquid handling robots give the  highest level of automation  for a water's lcm sms workflow  and to get the sample id and well  position information from the tcam into  a former that can be imported into mass  links  a file converter is used once the sample  extraction run is complete  the tcan evo wear software generates an  output file  containing the sample tube barcode and  final weld position information  the file converter monitors the folder  where this is saved  and any new files created are converted  to do this the software will take the  sample id and weld position  from the output file and merge it into a  method specific template file  which contains the lcm sms method  information  and this file can then be imported into  mass links  looking at the mass link slim's  interface in more detail  once drivers are created and set up in  collaboration with the lims provider  a connection between the two can be  established  to import data from a limbs which is  used to create mass link sample lists in  the piplus and android  workflows a message is received from the  lims for a particular test of a batch of  samples  from this the mass links limbs interface  combines the sample id  received from the lims with a method  specific  template containing the lcm sms method  settings  the analyst will then simply need to  specify the sample location  and submit the run for analysis  and as i've also mentioned the math link  sims interface tool  can be used with the hamilton system to  compare sample ids for a particular test  with the sample barcode scanned on deck  to ensure that the correct samples are  present for that  extraction run  and then once data is analyzed processed  and approved  the masslinx lim's interface can be used  to send data back to the limbs  almost all information that is present  in target links can be sent  where information is set up in the  software  and once a target links file is  processed and saved  it can be sent with the simple click of  a button  this removes any need to manually input  results back into a lim system  and therefore minimizing the risk of any  transcription errors  mass links and target links also contain  features to assist  in the acquiring and approval process  using masslink's basic security  and in this user privileged privileges  can be set for example groups are  created by an administrator  which is typically a lab director to  allow for different permissions  such as full system access for method  developers  and just run and process samples for  analysts  and using the windows operating system a  user account is created  and then assigned to a particular group  depending on their  training and job role  all actions that are then performed in  mass links are tracked  with an audit log being maintained  target links also has features to  require signatures and reasons for  changes  to integration for example and  signatures through reviewing and  accepting data sets  and so to summarize before i go through  an example  waters have seen that there is a need to  simplify the lcm sms workflows as much  as possible  and this is why we have tried to address  each of the bottlenecks  in the process from the request for a  sample analysis  right through to the reporting of  results back into the limbs  completing the circular sample journey  automating the workflow as much as  possible should help to improve  robustness and precision of the results  minimize human error and also minimize  transcription errors  so with my final few slides i just  wanted to go through an example  where we have used this workflow to  simplify the extraction and analysis  of a panel of steroid hormones within  our lab  and i think it nicely shows some of the  features that i've discussed  and also goes through a typical sample  extraction method for lcm sms  so for this testing i performed a  precision performance  experiment where i evaluated qc samples  at  low mid and high concentration levels  five replicas of each qc sample were  extracted and analyzed on each of five  occasions  along with a calibration line on the  hamilton star platform  analysis was performed on a water  security uplc  i-class c of otqs micro lcm sms system  and due to no limbs present in our lab  as we are not a production  site we use the limb simulator tool  to simulate this portion of the workflow  a test file was created using the  masslinx lims interface  which contained the method id  used to ensure the correct method is  loaded  and also a sample id information  this file was selected in the hamilton  method  as you can just about see in the top box  of the image  the file is also used for the system to  calculate the number of samples to  extract  and then the first available weld  position in the 96 well plate is chosen  which allows for partially used  extraction plates to be used  hamilton also allows for loading  instructions  which guide the analysts through loading  of the deck  and also barcode checking to ensure that  all consumables and reagents have been  loaded correctly  for example if the water reagent trough  had been loaded into position  two on the deck rather than position one  an error message will be received for  the analyst to change the position of  that trough to be correct  before continuing the run  all of these features are optional in  the method being displayed with a  checkbox as shown in the image  however it's also possible to lock these  down within the script itself  and here is just an example of a load  instruction from the method  where blue lights flash on the deck  image to identify the location to load  reagents and a window describing where  to load these reagents  and the minimum volumes required to  perform the extraction room  there are also lights that flash on the  front of the hamilton system  which display the correct tracks to add  the reagents  to so in the example shown in the image  above  you can just about see this will be on  track 14 of the deck  for the sample extraction of this method  we followed our application note  which is also available on the waters  website if you would like  further information following loading  and barcode scanning 100 microliters of  sample was transferred from the sample  tubes into a 96 well plate  where internal standard was added  followed by protein precipitation being  performed  the liquid handling robot mixed the  samples after each reagent addition  but centrifugation was performed off  deck  following this the samples were returned  to the liquid handling robot  and solid phase extraction performed  using an onboard vacuum system  once the extraction run was completed  the collection plate was transferred  manually into the lcm sms  for analysis and the mass linked sample  lists were created from the output file  generated directly by the hamilton  software  extracted samples were injected using  the acuity uplc  i-class ftn and samples separated  using an acuity uplc hss t3 column  with dimensions of 2.1 by 50 millimeters  and the vanguard pre-column detection  was performed  using the analytically sensitive xevo  tqs micro mass spectrometer  and the run time of five minutes  injection to injection  allowed for the samples the analysis of  12 samples per hour  once data was acquired it was processed  in target links  before statistical analysis was  performed using  analyze it in excel  the approximate hands-on time for the  analyst in this steroid workflow to  process  96 samples manually would take around  six hours to complete but utilizing the  described automation workflow solution  would only take approximately two hours  of hands-on analyst time to complete  allowing for other tasks to be performed  such as the  very important coffee breaks or even  lunch  so now let's come on to the results  concentrations of each steroid hormone  in the qc samples are shown in the table  when samples are extracted on the  hamilton across five days  total precision and repeatability was  less than 6.7 percent  at all concentrations for all of these  steroid hormones  showing the potential to provide a much  more precise  and robust workflow when compared to a  manual approach  whilst also reducing the risk of human  error and transcription errors  and so to summarize we have gone through  the reasons to focus on automation  where time-consuming and laborious steps  can be avoided  and human error and transcription errors  can be minimized  whilst also improving results across  days  with multiple analysts we have looked at  the current waters  automation portfolio for lcm sms  workflows  and taken the journey of the sample  through this workflow  before finally going through an example  for a steroid hormone  if you would like any more information  around what we have discussed today  then this can be found at warplus.com  forward slash clinical or alternatively  feel free to contact me on the email  address shown  and so with that i would like to thank  lab roots for allowing me to talk to you  today  and of course i thank you for your  attention and hope  you have found this presentation useful  thank you  you

