cm sms  so for this testing i performed a  precision performance  experiment where i evaluated qc samples  at  low mid and high concentration levels  five replicas of each qc sample were  extracted and analyzed on each of five  occasions  along with a calibration line on the  hamilton star platform  analysis was performed on a water  security uplc  i-class c of otqs micro lcm sms system  and due to no limbs present in our lab  as we are not a production  site we use the limb simulator tool  to simulate this portion of the workflow  a test file was created using the  masslinx lims interface  which contained the method id  used to ensure the correct method is  loaded  and also a sample id information  this file was selected in the hamilton  method  as you can just about see in the top box  of the image  the file is also used for the system to  calculate the number of samples to  extract  and then the first available weld  position in the 96 well plate is chosen  which allows for partially used  extraction plates to be used  hamilton also allows for loading  instructions  which guide the analysts through loading  of the deck  and also barcode checking to ensure that  all consumables and reagents have been  loaded correctly  for example if the water reagent trough  had been loaded into position  two on the deck rather than position one  an error message will be received for  the analyst to change the position of  that trough to be correct  before continuing the run  all of these features are optional in  the method being displayed with a  checkbox as shown in the image  however it's also possible to lock these  down within the script itself  and here is just an example of a load  instruction from the method  where blue lights flash on the deck  image to identify the location to load  reagents and a window describing where  to load these reagents  and the minimum volumes required to  perform the extraction room  there are also lights that flash on the  front of the hamilton system  which display the correct tracks to add  the reagents  to so in the example shown in the image  above  you can just about see this will be on  track 14 of the deck  for the sample extraction of this method  we followed our application note  which is also available on the waters  website if you would like  further information following loading  and barcode scanning 100 microliters of  sample was transferred from the sample  tubes into a 96 well plate  where internal standard was added  followed by protein precipitation being  performed  the liquid handling robot mixed the  samples after each reagent addition  but centrifugation was performed off  deck  following this the samples were returned  to the liquid handling robot  and solid phase extraction performed  using an onboard vacuum system  once the extraction run was completed  the collection plate was transferred  manually into the lcm sms  for analysis and the mass linked sample  lists were created from the output file  generated directly by the hamilton  software  extracted samples were injected using  the acuity uplc  i-class ftn and samples separated  using an acuity uplc hss t3 column  with dimensions of 2.1 by 50 millimeters  and the vanguard pre-column detection  was performed  using the analytically sensitive xevo  tqs micro mass spectrometer  and the run time of five minutes  injection to injection  allowed for the samples the analysis of  12 samples per hour  once data was acquired it was processed  in target links  before statistical analysis was  performed using  analyze it in excel  the approximate hands-on time for the  analyst in this steroid workflow to  process  96 samples manually would take around  six hours to complete but utilizing the  described automation workflow solution  would only take approximately two hours  of hands-on analyst time to complete  allowing for other tasks to be performed  such as the  very important coffee breaks or even  lunch  so now let's come on to the results  concentrations of each steroid hormone  in the qc samples are shown in the table  when samples are extracted on the  hamilton across five days  total precision and repeatability was  less than 6.7 percent  at all concentrations for all of these  steroid hormones  showing the potential to provide a much  more precise  and robust workflow when compared to a  manual approach  whilst also reducing the risk of human  error and transcription errors  and so to summarize we have gone through  the reasons to focus on automation  where time-consuming and laborious steps  can be avoided  and human error and transcription errors  can be minimized  whilst also improving results across  days  with multiple analysts we have looked at  the current waters  automation portfolio for lcm sms  workflows  and taken the journey of the sample  through this workflow  before finally going through an example  for a steroid hormone  if you would like any more information  around what we have discussed today  then this can be found at warplus.com  forward slash clinical or alternatively  feel free to contact me on the email  address shown  and so with that i would like to thank  lab roots for allowing me to talk to you  today  and of course i thank you for your  attention and hope  you have found this presentation useful  thank you  you
 

